sections.text:
In a chemical reaction atoms are neither made nor destroyed; they are only rearranged. So an equation must have the same number of atoms of each element on both sides.
The numbers written IN FRONT of the formulae are the coefficients. They may be changed freely. The small numbers INSIDE a formula are subscripts; they must never be changed, because they say what the substance is. Changing {formula('H2O')} to {formula('H2O2')} turns water into hydrogen peroxide.
A useful order:
{1}. balance the element that appears in the fewest places;
{2}. leave hydrogen and oxygen until last;
{3}. count every element again at the end.
